title ALES122 — Lotus's stereo_silence_span: silencing a span carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep
prompt Continue the recurring baton (prin recur) on the ALES/Lotus edge, Lindy-first crux-first; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates.
think Third lap of the session's recur run. After stereo_paste_over (ALES121) the tightest next crux is its zero-paint specialization: ALES110's mono silence_span, the length-preserving span-silence, had no stereo twin. Lifting it right after the overwrite paste lets the specialization law bind the two — silence is the overwrite whose paint is zero — proven against ALES121 directly.
think Read silence_span.rye (mono, faults BadRange only, length holds). Same lift shape as the others: check the shared span once up front, run mono silence_span per channel with the same start/count. Length holds so balance is preserved trivially.
think Wrote the design read, module, witness. Four laws proven on metal: stereo silence law (each channel == ALES110 byte for byte, span zeroed, rest unchanged), balance/length law (left.len == right.len, both holding length), specialization law (== ALES121's stereo_paste_over with zero payloads, side by side), atomicity/degenerate law (a refusal leaves both untouched, count 0 the identity, silence_span(0,len) silences both wholly, a span past samples BadRange).
obs The specialization law is the round's keystone — it ties silence_span to the paste_over landed one rung earlier over their public APIs, so the two can never drift apart. Witness GREEN first run; tame_style_check clean.
loom witness=ales_stereo_silence_span_witness result=GREEN laws=4 channels=2
